WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.580
Speaker 1: OK, let's get into audience questions
for the week, the first one, this is actually

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:05.920
a really interesting question.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.599
If getting out the vote is a valid strategy
for winning elections, why isn't suppressing

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:14.320
the vote a valid strategy?

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.900
We are talking about two different things
here, saying to people, you know what, none

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.990
of these candidates really have your interests
in mind.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:28.289
If you vote for anyone, you're reinforcing
some status quo or you're enabling the two

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:35.649
party system or whatever, even lying to people
about the positions of the candidates in order

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:38.260
to get them to dislike all of them and stay
home.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.710
You know that that's electioneering.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.340
I prefer people tell the truth.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:47.809
But the point is that that is a strategy to
say let's run a no vote campaign or a blank

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:50.670
vote campaign or whatever the case may be.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:58.510
That's the opposite of get out the vote, trying
to suppress turnout by lying about polling

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:06.680
places, lying about voter ID rules, pushing
voter purges, looking to limit early voting

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.570
hours or mail in voting.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.740
That is a completely different strategy.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:17.840
One is a strategy of disenfranchizing someone
from voting, which is a right.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.770
The other is just making the case that it's
not in your interests to vote.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.560
And if if people don't understand the difference
between these two things we're in, we're in

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:28.650
real trouble now.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.329
I would disagree with the strategy of four.

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:33.170
I mean, listen, we saw this.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.810
There were lots of people saying I don't like
any there were Bernie supporters who when

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.229
Hillary won or when Biden won, said, I don't
like Trump, but I don't like Biden.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:43.860
I don't like Trump, but I don't like Hillary.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.549
I just I'm opting out.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:46.549
I don't want to participate.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:48.899
I don't want to support any of these candidates.

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:49.979
I disagreed with them.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:53.689
But that's that's that's not voter suppression.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:02:01.020
That's saying I am making the decision to
wield my right to vote by not voting.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.020
Or voting third party or whatever the case
may be, those are people with whom we would

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.640
just talk and figure out, is that a good strategy?

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.640
What is the goal?

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:09.830
What are you trying to attain by doing that?

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.060
Will it actually work to attain that?

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.800
There is nothing similar when you then look
at what the Republican Party has done to try

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.610
to suppress the vote, trying to have absentee
ballots thrown out, limiting who can get absentee

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:29.720
ballots, cutting Sunday voting hours, purging
people because they haven't voted in a few

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.500
elections by saying these are no longer really
active voters in the sense that you should

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:41.740
be registered voter polling, intimidation,
that is disenfranchisement, convincing people

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.390
you are making the country better by not voting,
by choosing not to vote.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.910
I would disagree with the strategy.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.650
I think it's always better to vote in every
situation I've seen.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.320
But it is a completely different thing.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.410
And part of the problem is that when people
don't get it, they might be tricked by that

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.410
question.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.880
They might hear, hey, you know what?

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.340
It's true.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:10.030
If getting out the vote is a strategy, why
isn't suppressing the vote a strategy?

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:17.430
Well, suppressing by just saying choose not
to vote, protest with us, protest by not voting.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:22.470
That's totally legitimate suppressing in the
way that Republicans have been trying to do.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:27.490
We now have over 250 voter suppression bills
across more than 40 states.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.490
Why?

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.190
Because they didn't suppress enough of the
vote to win in 2020.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:33.190
It's quite that simple.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.600
A very different thing.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.750
I'm I'm glad to answer the question.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:51.040
There is very little comparison 

00:03:51.040 --> 00:04:47.030
between 

00:04:47.030 --> 00:05:01.450
the two.

